WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.423
Welcome back. Today we are looking at TCP resets and how to find out who actually sent them.

2
00:00:07.623 --> 00:00:14.546
A reset is a TCP segment with the RST flag set, and it tears the connection down immediately.

3
00:00:14.746 --> 00:00:20.515
Unlike a FIN, a reset does not wait for the other side to acknowledge anything.

4
00:00:20.715 --> 00:00:25.715
The first thing I do is apply the display filter tcp.flags.reset == 1.

5
00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:33.223
That shows only the reset packets, so in this capture we go from forty thousand frames down to twelve.

6
00:00:33.423 --> 00:00:40.346
Next, look at the IP time to live on the reset compared with the rest of that conversation.

7
00:00:40.546 --> 00:00:49.392
If the server normally arrives with a TTL of fifty-two and the reset arrives with sixty-four, something in the middle probably injected it.

8
00:00:49.592 --> 00:00:56.130
Firewalls and load balancers often send resets on behalf of the server when a session idles out.

9
00:00:56.330 --> 00:01:03.253
In this case the culprit was a firewall with an idle timeout of three hundred and sixty seconds.

10
00:01:03.453 --> 00:01:10.376
The application kept its connection open for about ten minutes between requests, so the firewall dropped the state.

11
00:01:10.576 --> 00:01:17.114
When the client finally sent data, the firewall had no session entry and replied with a reset.

12
00:01:17.314 --> 00:01:22.314
The fix was to enable TCP keepalives on the client every sixty seconds.

13
00:01:22.514 --> 00:01:29.822
Another common pattern is a reset right after the SYN, which simply means nothing is listening on that port.

14
00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:36.945
You will see SYN, then RST ACK from the server, and the whole exchange takes under a millisecond.

15
00:01:37.145 --> 00:01:43.299
That is not a network problem, it is a closed port or a service that crashed.

16
00:01:43.499 --> 00:01:50.037
A third pattern is the application itself calling close with unread data still in the receive buffer.

17
00:01:50.237 --> 00:01:56.775
Linux sends a reset instead of a FIN in that situation, which surprises a lot of developers.

18
00:01:56.975 --> 00:02:03.513
To spot it, check whether the reset follows a data packet that the receiving application never read.

19
00:02:03.713 --> 00:02:10.251
I also add a column for the TCP stream index so I can jump between conversations quickly.

20
00:02:10.451 --> 00:02:14.682
Right click the stream index field and choose apply as column.

21
00:02:14.882 --> 00:02:21.420
Finally, open Statistics, then Conversations, and sort by the number of packets to find the busiest flows.

22
00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:28.158
If resets cluster in one flow, the problem is usually local to that client and server pair.

23
00:02:28.358 --> 00:02:36.050
If they are spread across every flow at the same timestamp, suspect a shared device, for example a firewall reboot.

24
00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:42.019
That covers the three patterns I check first: idle timeouts, closed ports, and abortive closes.

25
00:02:42.219 --> 00:02:48.373
Thanks for watching, and grab the sample capture from the description if you want to practice.
